Subject: [PATCH 0/4] SEAMCALL helpers for TDX PCIe Link Encryption

This posting is just to collect initial review. They are all
x86/virt/tdx changes, so Dave, Krill, Rick and Dan please take a look.

This series adds SEAMCALL helpers in preparation for PCIe link
encryption on TDX platforms. It is part of the TDX PCIe Link Encryption
feature but is split out for 2 purposes:

 1. The SEAMCALL helpers are relatively independent so the splitting
    reduces the size of the Link Encryption series, making it easier to
    review.

 2. Invite earlier discussion on the abstraction of HPA_LIST_INFO, which
    is used across multiple SEAMCALLs for TDX module extensions, TDX
    TDISP and TD migration.

== Overview ==

TDISP (TEE Device Interface Security Protocol) is a PCI-SIG standard
for link encryption & trust establishment between CoCo-VM and assigned
devices, allowing CoCo-VM to accept devices for private memory access
(private DMA), improving I/O performance for Confidential
Computing (CC). This series focuses on the link encryption part of the
TDX TDISP feature.

Two protocols, SPDM (Security Protocol and Data Model) and PCI
IDE (Integrity and Data Encryption) work together to establish the link
encryption. SPDM creates trust on untrusted transit for key exchange.
IDE performs the actual real-time encryption for data traffic. In the CC
world, they are managed by secure firmware. On TDX platforms, the TDX
module plays this role, and the host drives these operations through
these newly introduced SEAMCALLs.

One major problem to solve in this series is the generic abstraction for
the SEAMCALL parameter type - HPA_LIST_INFO. This is a TDX module
defined structure to carry multi-page memory exchanges between the host
and TDX module. This concept is first introduced in TDX module
extensions initialization but is described by separate parameters, a
page of HPA list entries and an entry count. This pattern would bloat
the parameter list and diverge from the TDX module ABI as it appears in
more and more SEAMCALL helpers.

A struct tdx_hpa_list_info is introduced to consolidate the parameters
for SEAMCALL helpers. Several helpers are also introduced to setup/free
the tdx_hpa_list_info structure in a generic way.

== Some history ==

There was another page list structure defined in TDX module ABI -
HPA_ARRAY_T - which is similar to HPA_LIST_INFO but has an extra
"singleton mode" optimization. Recent discussions show the optimization
is premature so HPA_ARRAY_T is removed from TDX module ABI. It is either
replaced by HPA_LIST_INFO for multi-page cases, or by an HPA for
single-page cases.

Some destroy SEAMCALLs (such as TDH.SPDM.DELETE) previously required the
host to provide a container page (called AUX page in TDX module ABI) to
receive an HPA list of released pages. Linux doesn't rely on this
mechanism to track memory lifecycle so the ABI is changed to allow for a
NULL_PA (U64_MAX) input.

These 2 ABI changes are not published yet, so may cause confusing when
refering to ABI spec.

== References ==

The HPA_LIST_INFO definition can be found at "Intel TDX Module ABI
Specification" [1], Section 3.5.3

The SEAMCALL definitions for TDX TDISP can be found at "Intel TDX
Connect ABI Specification" [2].

The SEAMCALL definitions for TD Migration can be found at "Intel TDX
Module TD Migration ABI Specification" [3].

This series is based on the TDX module Extensions Initialization, which
is part of the "DICE-based TDX Quoting Extension" series [4]. This
series doesn't include the actual TDX Link Encryption implementation
which is the user of these exported helpers. Those patches will be sent
later. A full preview of all these dependencies and follow-ups can be
found at [5].

